I love playing knights and jousting, but I hate it when my lance
breaks.  Here's my proposal for an artifact lance:

A silver lance named "Wyrmslayer"

Applied: +5 to hit, +5 damage (1d6+5 vs small / 1d8+5 vs Large)
Wielded (mounted): +5 to hit, +5 damage (1d6+5 vs small / 1d8+5 vs
Large)
Wielded (not mounted): 1d2 vs small & Large

Specials:
x2 damage vs Dragons
x2 vs silver-hating monsters

15% chance (based on your to-hit roll) of cancelling a dragon.
      "Wyrmslayer shines brilliantly!" if you can see or
      "Wyrmslayer roars deafeningly!" if you can't
      and "It strikes <monster>!"
    The dragon may resist cancellation.

+2d10 for a successful joust (2d2 if with a secondary weapon). This
bonus can't be doubled. To joust successfully, you need to hit with a
lance in melee while riding (and not fumbling or stunned), and the
chance is dependent on your skill in lance (or, if two-weaponing, the
lower of your skill in two-weapon combat and in lance): 20% at
unskilled, 40% at basic, 60% at skilled, 80% at expert. The monster
will be stunned for a turn and thrown back a square (if possible).
Unlike normal lances, Wyrmslayer is *unbreakable* and will never
shatter from successful jousting!

***

This weapon will normally do less damage than Reaper (Slashem Yeoman
artifact polearm), but on a successful joust against Dragons and
silver-haters, it will generally edge out that crazy +20 damage bonus
that Reaper has.

This idea is an homage to the idea of knights in general as
dragonslayers, as well as the Dragonlance series in particular.



